The Security Council,

Recalling the Protocol for the Prohibition of the Use in War of Asphyxiating, Poisonous or Other Gases, and of Bacteriological Methods of Warfare, signed at Geneva on 17 June 1925, and the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on Their Destruction (CWC),

Recalling its resolutions 1540 (2004), 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2314 (2016) and 2319 (2016),

Reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons constitutes a serious violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,

Condemning in the strongest terms any use of toxic chemicals as weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic by anyone, and emphasizing that those responsible for any such acts must be held accountable,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic,

Underlining the necessity of establishing a genuinely impartial, independent, professional and objective mechanism to investigate allegations of the use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, free from politicization and preconceived conclusions,

Stressing that any credible investigation must strictly adhere to the standards and provisions of the Chemical Weapons Convention, particularly with regard to the verification annex, the integrity of the chain of custody, and the collection of first-hand physical, environmental and biomedical samples through direct on-site visits,

1. Decides to establish a United Nations Independent Mechanism of Investigation (UNIMI) for a period of one year from the date of the adoption of this resolution, with the possibility of extension by the Security Council, to identify to the greatest extent feasible individuals, entities, groups, or governments who were perpetrators, organizers, sponsors or otherwise involved in the use of chemicals as weapons, including chlorine or any other toxic chemical, in the Syrian Arab Republic;

2. Requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the Director-General of the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (OPCW), to submit to the Security Council within 30 days of the adoption of this resolution, for its approval, recommendations regarding the terms of reference and operational modalities of the UNIMI, ensuring broad and balanced geographical representation in its leadership and expert personnel;

3. Decides that the UNIMI shall carry out its mandate in strict compliance with the highest standards of the Chemical Weapons Convention, basing its findings exclusively on verified, incontrovertible, and conclusive evidence obtained through mandatory on-site investigations at the locations of alleged incidents, direct sampling, and rigorous adherence to chain-of-custody protocols;

4. Directs that, in circumstances where security conditions prevent immediate access to an incident site, the UNIMI shall report the situation to the Security Council and refrain from drawing definitive conclusions or attributing responsibility based solely on remote testimony, unverified open-source materials, or information provided by third parties and partisan actors;

5. Decides that the UNIMI shall report to the Security Council on its findings, and affirms that the Security Council retains the sole prerogative to assess the credibility and sufficiency of the evidence presented and to determine appropriate subsequent measures under the Charter of the United Nations;

6. Calls upon the Government of the Syrian Arab Republic and all parties in Syria to cooperate fully with the UNIMI and the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission (FFM), including by facilitating prompt, safe, and unimpeded access to all relevant sites, witnesses, and documentation;

7. Calls upon all Member States to provide any relevant information in their possession to the UNIMI to facilitate the fulfillment of its mandate;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

